<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Rizal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Rizal was a Filipino physician, writer and reformer, and the national hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. His novels Noli Me Tángere (1887) and El filibusterismo (1891) exposed the abuses of Spanish colonial rule and the friars. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> in 1892, he was arrested after the outbreak of the Philippine Revolution, tried for rebellion and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> on 30 December 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-rizal.jpg" alt="José Rizal" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jose_Rizal_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Protasio Rizal Mercado y Alonso Realonda was born on 19 June 1861. His family took Rizal as a second surname after Governor-General Narciso Clavería decreed in 1849 that Filipinos adopt Spanish surnames from a list, and continued to use Mercado. The family's patrilineal line traced back to Lam-co, a Chinese merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> who settled in Calamba in 1697.</p><p>In May 1882, without his parents' knowledge, but secretly supported by his brother Paciano and his uncle Antonio, he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to study. He became an ophthalmologist by profession and a key member of the Propaganda Movement of the 1880s, which advocated political reforms for the colony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rizal wrote mostly in Spanish. His essays include El amor patrio of 1882 and Filipinas dentro de cien años, 1889–90, and he annotated Antonio de Morga's Sucesos de las Islas Filipinas, published in 1890 after he moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. His novels Noli Me Tángere (1887) and its sequel El filibusterismo (1891) are together taken as a national epic. His poetry includes Mi último adiós, written in 1896.</p><p>On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1892 he formed La Liga Filipina, which advocated moderate social reforms through legal means but was disbanded by the governor. On 6 July 1892 he was arrested for alleged possession of a seditious document and banished to Dapitan on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>. There he built a school, a hospital and a water supply system, taught, and farmed. In February 1895 he met Josephine Bracken, an Irish woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, who later became his common-law wife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With Governor General Ramón Blanco's leave to serve as a doctor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, Rizal left Dapitan on 1 August 1896. By then the Katipunan's rebellion had become a nationwide revolution. At a stopover in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> he was arrested on 6 October 1896. He was executed for rebellion on 30 December 1896 by a squad of Filipino soldiers of the Spanish Army, with a backup force of regular troops ready to shoot the executioners if they refused orders. Though not involved in the revolution's planning or conduct, he ultimately approved of its goals. His last words were reported as consummatum est, it is finished.</p><p>Rizal is widely considered one of the most influential figures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and has been recommended as a national hero by an officially empaneled National Heroes Committee. No law, executive order or proclamation has officially proclaimed any Filipino historical figure a national hero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Rizal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Constitution of the United States inspired constitution-makers well beyond those who copied its presidency, from Benito Juárez in Mexico to <b>José Rizal</b> in the Philippines and Sun Yat-sen in China, although since the 1980s new constitutions have more often looked elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nationalism grew after the Cavite mutiny of 1872, and the execution of <b>Jose Rizal</b> in 1896 fed the Philippine Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.6,120.98&amp;year=1883" aria-label="Manila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of José Rizal"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5081 3765h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5081 3765h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=14.6,120.98&amp;year=1883">What was here in 1883? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Where are the youth who will consecrate their golden hours, their illusions, and their enthusiasm to the welfare of their native land?&#8221;</p><footer>The Reign of Greed (El Filibusterismo, 1891) &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; spoken by Padre Florentino &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10676/pg10676-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Recorded in the history of human sufferings is a cancer of so malignant a character that the least touch irritates it and awakens in it the sharpest pains.&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Cancer (Noli Me Tangere, 1887), dedication To My Fatherland &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6737/pg6737-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I die without seeing the dawn brighten over my native land! You, who have it to see, welcome it—and forget not those who have fallen during the night!&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Cancer (Noli Me Tangere, 1887), ch. 63 &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; spoken by Elías as he dies &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6737/pg6737-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…since we mortals in general are very much like tortoises: we are esteemed and classified according to our shells…&#8221;</p><footer>The Social Cancer (Noli Me Tangere, 1887), ch. 2 &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6737/pg6737-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why independence, if the slaves of today will be the tyrants of tomorrow? And that they will be such is not to be doubted, for he who submits to tyranny loves it.&#8221;</p><footer>The Reign of Greed (El Filibusterismo, 1891), ch. 39 &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; spoken by Padre Florentino &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10676/pg10676-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In order to read the destiny of a people, it is necessary to open the book of its past…&#8221;</p><footer>The Philippines a Century Hence (1889) &#183; tr. Charles Derbyshire, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35899/pg35899-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
